        <body>
            <div xml:id="d614">

                <pb facs="0853" n="773" sameAs="frus:frus1936v01/pg773"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">647.116/249a: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State to the Consul General at
                        Sydney</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Moffat</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1936-11-18T18:00:00-05:00">November 18,
                            1936—6 p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Representative Maas of Minnesota has brought to me a letter from Brandtjen and
                    Kluge regarding shipments of printing presses which have arrived at Sydney,
                    Melbourne and Adelaide and stating that their agents, S. Cooke Proprietary
                    Limited of Melbourne, have been unable to obtain entry permits.</p>
                <p>I would like your opinion whether the special circumstances of these machines
                    having already been unladen in Australia might not, upon special representations
                    being made by you, be given consideration. In general, do you think that special
                    representations in specific cases would be of avail in securing import
                    permits.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Moore</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
